"Positive I leaves the + terminal of the source and circulates the right hand way around +z: counterclockwise seen from the right, so B inside points along +z. For the toroid, positive I makes B circulate counterclockwise seen from above (the page). Φ<sub>B</sub> is counted along that B, so NΦ<sub>B</sub> = LI with L > {#}. A positive ε would drive a positive current. With these choices ε = −N dΦ<sub>B</sub>/dt = −L dI/dt, and the source must supply V = L dI/dt = −ε across the coil; the + and − marks at the coil show that voltage.",
"Curl your fingers along the current in the turns and your thumb points along B inside. The back EMF always opposes the change in I: it points against I while |I| grows and along I while |I| shrinks.",
"Solenoid: an ideal long solenoid, B = μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀(N/ℓ)I uniform inside and zero outside, so L = μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀N²A/ℓ with A = πr² (the OpenStax result, with a core). This ignores the ends, where the field spreads out and weakens. For comparison the rail also gives the air core value with the ends included: L = Kμ₀N²A/ℓ, with Nagaoka's coefficient K for a single layer coil taken as a uniform current sheet (Lorenz's closed form with complete elliptic integrals; Rosa and Grover {#}; Grover, <i>Inductance Calculations</i>, {#}; it agrees with a direct integral of Maxwell's ring formula over the sheet to better than {#}<sup>−{#}</sup>). The simulation uses the ideal value. Core: linear (B = μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀H, no saturation, no hysteresis, no eddy currents) and filling the whole inside. For an open solenoid that is not very long this overstates a real core a lot, because the poles at its ends oppose its magnetization; the closed toroid has no ends, so there μ<sub>r</sub> applies as written. Toroid: rectangular cross section (inner radius a, outer b, height h), turns close together, B = μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀NI/({#}πρ) inside and zero elsewhere, L = μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀N²h ln(b/a)/({#}π) exactly for that winding. Circuit: an ideal current source forces I(t); the coil has no resistance and no capacitance between turns. Each {#} ms step gives ε = −LΔI/Δt, exact for a linear ramp; the source's work ∫(−ε)I dt equals Δ(½LI²) exactly. The field energy check integrates B²/({#}μ<sub>r</sub>μ₀) over the volume holding the field. A real switch cannot stop a current in an instant: ε = −L dI/dt would be enormous and a spark would jump the gap, so here a switch takes {#} ms. The drawing shows one loop for every {#} turns (at least four); the field arrows show its direction, and fade with |I|.",
